# meta-deploy.list: the queue meta-deploy.bash runs, in this order.
#
# STANDING RULE (CLAUDE/AgentNotes.md): this list is exactly what needs running NOW. Add a
# run when it is pending, remove it once it has run and is done, leave out what cannot
# usefully run yet, then ask the owner to run meta-deploy.bash. Every step a run needs lives
# in the plan's own scripts, so this is the only command the owner is handed. The order is by
# dependency. A plan with none of triage.bash, deploy.bash and acceptance.bash has nothing
# for the script to do. A triage-only plan is run once, read-only. Words after the folder
# name are passed to that plan's triage.bash, both runs.
#
# One entry per line, four kinds:
#   <plan folder>                  its triage, deploy and acceptance scripts, in order. The
#                                  folder is named as it sits in CLAUDE/Plan/, with no
#                                  CLAUDE/Plan/ in front (00123-name, not CLAUDE/Plan/00123-name)
#   playbooks/<path>.yml [args]    a change no plan owns; run as one unit through its own
#                                  shebang, which goes through run.bash, exactly as
#                                  ./playbooks/imports/<play>.yml does by hand. Words after
#                                  it are ansible-playbook arguments: name the play's tags
#                                  (--tags lightning) so only the changed part runs
#   <plan folder>/<script>.bash    one plan script, for a step none of the three fixed names
#                                  fits; run as one unit, like a playbook entry, except
#                                  that a script named *acceptance*.bash may exit 2 for
#                                  COULD NOT ESTABLISH, as an acceptance.bash may
#   changed-plays                  every play that has run here and has to run again, as
#                                  one unit: the checkout's ./run.bash --changed --yes, in
#                                  playbook-main.yml order, stopping at the first failure
#
# changed-plays runs WHERE IT STANDS in the list, like every other entry, and decides which
# plays it runs when it starts, so the entries above it have already run (a play an entry
# above ran is no longer changed, and is not run twice). Put it above the plan entries whose
# deploy or acceptance expects the changed plays to have run, which is the usual case, and
# below an entry it must follow. It replaces a hand-copied list of stale plays: name a
# playbooks/ entry only for a play that must run at a fixed point between two plan steps.
# Its description says which changed plays the agent expects it to pick up and why.
#
# A line starting with # is a comment and is the description of the entry below it: it is how
# the owner learns what a run will do, so write it. Blank lines are ignored. Any other line
# that is not an entry is a fatal error naming its line number.

# Issues #284, #290, #293 and Plan 00254 (Task 4.4, Tasks 5.2 to 5.4): ccy 3.122.7, container
# 2.84.18. Installs the launcher and rebuilds ccy-sessions and ccy-teams; the image is rebuilt
# once, because it carries the agent-bus Element helper, which now accepts a team entry's
# `ca_file` and `client`, and the Matrix client list, which now names Lightning's per-team folder. #284: restore --check (and declare, and the
# self-update reboot hold) also refuses a gone --ssh-key or --network, an unusable engine, an
# allowed-hostnames that rules this host out, an unusable restore passphrase file and an
# all-expired cc token pool; ccy-teams restart names a refused start. #290: the new wind-down
# verb (every live session finishes its turn and compacts while its prompt cache is warm), and
# a set-going that types continue into every restored session, never /compact. #293: an
# allowed-hostnames file that cannot be read stops the launch, and every launcher library loop
# that reads a file checks that it could open it. ccy-teams also now says, where it cannot read
# a team's members without a password, that host_vars may keep the sudoers rule off.
# Afterwards run `ccy-sessions restore --check` and report its lines.
playbooks/imports/play-claude-yolo.yml

# Issue #290: the self-update cycle now runs ccy-sessions wind-down after its one-minute
# warning and reboots when it returns (ten-minute deadline), recording any session not
# compacted. Run after the play above, so the verb the cycle calls is installed.
playbooks/imports/optional/common/play-self-update.yml

# Issue #296: rebuilds and reinstalls teamchat-admin with the human verbs (human password,
# logout, devices), so a declared human of a v2 team can be given a password and sign in from
# Lightning. Only the admin tag: no unit, team or Tuwunel change. Afterwards, for each human:
# sudo teamchat-admin human password --team <team> --name <name>
playbooks/imports/play-teamchat.yml --tags admin

# Plan 00254 D1: Lightning is brought to Flathub's latest release on every run, changed only
# when it was updated. No release carries our upstream fixes yet, so this run should report no
# change for Lightning; it proves the new step on the host.
playbooks/imports/optional/common/play-matrix-clients.yml --tags lightning

# Issue #293: agent-bus-install checks that each file it loops over (the Tuwunel pins, the
# firewall rule record, the homeserver unit) could be opened, instead of reading an unopenable
# one as empty. Expected: changed only for agent-bus-install itself. This run also honours
# agent_bus_read_only_sudoers: a host that sets it false in host_vars has
# /etc/sudoers.d/agent-bus-read-only removed; a host that sets nothing sees no change there.
playbooks/imports/play-agent-bus.yml

# Issue #293: lan-scan reads its scan results from a here-string instead of a file loop.
# Expected: changed only for lan-scan.
playbooks/imports/optional/common/play-network-tools.yml

# Issue #293: the VM lab's copies of the server-github-token acceptance checker and of
# secret-scan.bash (the run-log scrubber's engine) check that each file they loop over could be
# opened. Expected: changed only for those two files; skip this entry on a host without the lab.
playbooks/imports/optional/common/play-vm-test-lab.yml

# Plan 00254 Tasks 5.2 to 5.4: the agent-bus Element play learns which client each team opens
# in (its client:, else agent_bus_matrix_client, else element). With nothing set this host
# changes nothing for Element. It builds and runs the new agent-bus-lightning command, which
# for a lightning team writes its own instance under
# ~/.var/app/org.lightning_matrix.Lightning/teams/<team>/, a homeserver settings file and an
# "<team> team (Lightning)" launcher; with no lightning team it writes no instance. Lightning's
# CA step now takes only lightning teams, so with none it removes the team CAs an earlier run put
# in ~/.var/app/org.lightning_matrix.Lightning/teamchat-ca/ and reports changed; Element's CA
# trust is unchanged. Afterwards report what changed, and any instance the play shows as kept.
# The plan's acceptance (the next entry) judges what this run wrote.
playbooks/imports/optional/common/play-agent-bus-element.yml --tags agent-bus-element,agent-bus-lightning

# Plan 00254 Task 5.5: the verdict on the play above, read-only, as the desktop user, no sudo.
# For each team that opens in Lightning: its instance folders (mode 0700), its settings file
# (0600, naming the team's homeserver) and its launcher (the exact Exec line), no Element
# launcher beside it, and for an https team its CA in Lightning's CA folder and a handshake from
# Lightning's sandbox; for each Element team, no Lightning launcher. It starts no window. On a
# host where no team opens in Lightning it exits 2, COULD NOT ESTABLISH (nothing to check),
# which is expected until a team is set to lightning. Afterwards report its PASS and FAIL lines
# and the run directory it names, and check the owner's list it prints at the end (each window
# opens on its own login page with the homeserver filled in; signing in works).
00254-lightning-default-team-client/acceptance.bash
